1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,539
A drop of ink spreads through water.

2
00:00:02,539 --> 00:00:04,536
A pricked finger bleeds bright red.

3
00:00:04,536 --> 00:00:06,541
A long run makes you breathe hard.

4
00:00:06,941 --> 00:00:10,217
All three are linked to how
oxygen moves inside you.

5
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:13,230
Diya pricked her finger
in the biology lab.

6
00:00:13,230 --> 00:00:16,898
She asked: how does oxygen get
from my lungs into this blood?

7
00:00:16,898 --> 00:00:19,471
And how does blood carry it to every cell?

8
00:00:19,821 --> 00:00:21,735
Here is the short answer.

9
00:00:21,735 --> 00:00:25,393
Gases move by diffusion,
from high pressure to low.

10
00:00:25,393 --> 00:00:30,083
Blood carries oxygen mostly on
haemoglobin, and carbon dioxide mostly as

11
00:00:30,083 --> 00:00:31,050
bicarbonate.

12
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:36,142
By the end, you will know partial
pressure, the diffusion membrane, gas

13
00:00:36,142 --> 00:00:38,935
transport and the oxygen
dissociation curve.

14
00:00:39,585 --> 00:00:41,052
First, a new word.

15
00:00:41,052 --> 00:00:42,253
Partial pressure.

16
00:00:42,603 --> 00:00:44,331
Air is a mix of gases.

17
00:00:44,331 --> 00:00:49,122
The part of the pressure that comes
from one gas is its partial pressure.

18
00:00:49,122 --> 00:00:53,317
We write p O 2 for oxygen,
and p CO 2 for carbon dioxide.

19
00:00:53,767 --> 00:00:58,068
Gases diffuse from where their partial
pressure is high to where it is

20
00:00:58,068 --> 00:00:58,968
low.

21
00:00:58,575 --> 00:01:03,253
Carbon dioxide also dissolves twenty to
twenty-five times better than oxygen.

22
00:01:03,903 --> 00:01:06,302
Let's zoom into the place of exchange.

23
00:01:06,652 --> 00:01:10,925
Here is one alveolus, wrapped in a net
of tiny blood vessels called capillaries.

24
00:01:11,425 --> 00:01:14,489
Zoom to where the alveolus
touches a capillary.

25
00:01:14,489 --> 00:01:18,130
Only a very thin wall separates
the air from the blood.

26
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:19,530
Zoom in.

27
00:01:19,391 --> 00:01:24,252
This is the diffusion membrane, with
three layers: the thin squamous epithelium

28
00:01:24,252 --> 00:01:29,015
of the alveolus, the endothelium of the
capillary, and the basement substance

29
00:01:29,015 --> 00:01:30,023
between them.

30
00:01:30,023 --> 00:01:32,651
Together, less than a millimetre thick.

31
00:01:33,301 --> 00:01:36,961
Now follow one oxygen
molecule, step by step.

32
00:01:37,311 --> 00:01:38,211
Step one.

33
00:01:38,073 --> 00:01:42,861
In the alveolus, p O 2 is high, one
hundred and four millimetres of mercury.

34
00:01:43,611 --> 00:01:44,511
Step two.

35
00:01:44,279 --> 00:01:46,884
The blood arriving has
low p O 2, only forty.

36
00:01:46,884 --> 00:01:48,765
So oxygen diffuses into the blood.

37
00:01:49,515 --> 00:01:50,415
Step three.

38
00:01:50,326 --> 00:01:54,532
In a red blood cell, oxygen binds to
haemoglobin and forms oxyhaemoglobin.

39
00:01:54,532 --> 00:01:57,652
Each haemoglobin can carry
up to four oxygen molecules.

40
00:01:58,402 --> 00:01:59,302
Step four.

41
00:01:59,249 --> 00:02:02,565
Blood leaving the lungs
has a p O 2 of ninety-five.

42
00:02:02,565 --> 00:02:06,086
It flows to the tissues,
where p O 2 is only forty.

43
00:02:06,836 --> 00:02:07,736
Step five.

44
00:02:07,607 --> 00:02:10,879
Oxygen leaves the haemoglobin
and diffuses into the cells.

45
00:02:11,629 --> 00:02:12,690
Remember this.

46
00:02:12,690 --> 00:02:16,052
Gases always move from high
partial pressure to low.

47
00:02:16,652 --> 00:02:18,944
Now the most famous graph in this chapter.

48
00:02:19,294 --> 00:02:23,895
Plot p O 2 against the percent
saturation of haemoglobin with oxygen.

49
00:02:23,895 --> 00:02:27,267
The curve is sigmoid,
shaped like the letter S.

50
00:02:27,267 --> 00:02:29,880
This is the oxygen dissociation curve.

51
00:02:30,330 --> 00:02:35,549
In the alveoli, high p O 2, low p CO
2, fewer hydrogen ions and a lower

52
00:02:35,549 --> 00:02:37,562
temperature help oxygen bind.

53
00:02:37,562 --> 00:02:42,781
In the tissues, low p O 2, high p CO
2, more hydrogen ions and a higher

54
00:02:42,781 --> 00:02:44,960
temperature make oxygen let go.

55
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:48,060
Now compare how blood
carries the two gases.

56
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:49,310
Oxygen.

57
00:02:49,056 --> 00:02:53,265
About ninety-seven percent rides
in red blood cells, on haemoglobin.

58
00:02:53,265 --> 00:02:57,640
Only three percent is dissolved in
plasma, the liquid part of blood.

59
00:02:58,090 --> 00:02:59,283
Carbon dioxide.

60
00:02:59,283 --> 00:03:04,066
Twenty to twenty-five percent rides on
haemoglobin as carbamino-haemoglobin.

61
00:03:04,066 --> 00:03:07,112
About seventy percent
travels as bicarbonate.

62
00:03:07,112 --> 00:03:10,116
About seven percent is
dissolved in plasma.

63
00:03:10,766 --> 00:03:13,570
How does carbon dioxide
become bicarbonate?

64
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:17,985
Red blood cells are full of an
enzyme called carbonic anhydrase.

65
00:03:17,985 --> 00:03:22,713
At the tissues, it joins carbon dioxide
and water into carbonic acid, which

66
00:03:22,713 --> 00:03:25,393
splits into bicarbonate and hydrogen ions.

67
00:03:25,843 --> 00:03:30,061
At the lungs, the same reaction runs
backwards, and carbon dioxide is set

68
00:03:30,061 --> 00:03:31,638
free to be breathed out.

69
00:03:31,638 --> 00:03:35,702
Every hundred millilitres of blood
delivers about five millilitres of oxygen

70
00:03:35,702 --> 00:03:39,226
to the tissues, and four of
carbon dioxide to the alveoli.

71
00:03:39,826 --> 00:03:40,726
Your turn.

72
00:03:40,709 --> 00:03:43,237
In the tissues, p CO 2 is high.

73
00:03:43,237 --> 00:03:46,892
Does that help haemoglobin
hold oxygen, or let it go?

74
00:03:46,892 --> 00:03:48,192
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:51,392 --> 00:03:52,292
It lets go!

76
00:03:52,292 --> 00:03:56,460
High p CO 2 makes oxygen leave
haemoglobin, right where the cells need

77
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:57,360
it.

78
00:03:57,532 --> 00:03:59,062
Now a worked example.

79
00:03:59,062 --> 00:04:02,083
Which way does each gas
move at the alveolus?

80
00:04:02,433 --> 00:04:03,333
Oxygen.

81
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:06,566
Alveolus one hundred and
four, blood arriving forty.

82
00:04:06,566 --> 00:04:10,556
The difference is sixty-four,
so oxygen moves into the blood.

83
00:04:11,006 --> 00:04:12,204
Carbon dioxide.

84
00:04:12,204 --> 00:04:15,090
Blood arriving forty-five, alveolus forty.

85
00:04:15,090 --> 00:04:20,308
The difference is only five, so carbon
dioxide moves out into the alveolus.

86
00:04:20,758 --> 00:04:22,633
Why is a gap of five enough?

87
00:04:22,633 --> 00:04:26,719
Carbon dioxide dissolves twenty to
twenty-five times better, so it diffuses

88
00:04:26,719 --> 00:04:28,540
fast even with a small push.

89
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:30,652
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:04:31,002 --> 00:04:34,817
Many students think most carbon
dioxide travels on haemoglobin.

91
00:04:35,217 --> 00:04:38,570
But about seventy percent
travels as bicarbonate.

92
00:04:38,570 --> 00:04:42,793
Only twenty to twenty-five percent
rides as carbamino-haemoglobin.

93
00:04:43,443 --> 00:04:44,863
Where is this asked?

94
00:04:44,863 --> 00:04:49,199
As statement questions on partial
pressures, the percentages of transport,

95
00:04:49,199 --> 00:04:52,295
and the factors that help
oxygen bind or let go.

96
00:04:52,895 --> 00:04:54,046
Exam level now.

97
00:04:54,046 --> 00:04:55,742
Read the four statements.

98
00:04:55,742 --> 00:04:56,941
Pause and solve.

99
00:05:02,341 --> 00:05:03,544
The answer is C.

100
00:05:03,544 --> 00:05:06,266
Statements one, three
and four are correct.

101
00:05:06,766 --> 00:05:11,447
Alveolar p O 2 is one hundred and four,
red blood cells are rich in carbonic

102
00:05:11,447 --> 00:05:13,840
anhydrase, and the curve is sigmoid.

103
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:16,251
Statement two is wrong.

104
00:05:16,251 --> 00:05:21,391
Ninety-seven percent of oxygen travels
on haemoglobin in red blood cells,

105
00:05:21,391 --> 00:05:22,708
not in plasma.

106
00:05:23,308 --> 00:05:24,848
Here is your recap.

107
00:05:24,848 --> 00:05:26,431
Screenshot this card.

108
00:05:29,631 --> 00:05:31,811
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:05:32,211 --> 00:05:33,111
One.

110
00:05:32,635 --> 00:05:34,080
What is partial pressure?

111
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:35,880
Two.

112
00:05:35,417 --> 00:05:38,103
Name the three layers of
the diffusion membrane.

113
00:05:39,003 --> 00:05:39,903
Three.

114
00:05:39,546 --> 00:05:42,154
How is most carbon
dioxide carried in blood?

115
00:05:43,254 --> 00:05:48,088
Next time: why do you breathe faster
when carbon dioxide builds up, and

116
00:05:48,088 --> 00:05:50,041
what goes wrong in asthma?

117
00:05:50,041 --> 00:05:54,474
We find out in regulation of
respiration and respiratory disorders.
